WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
THE STATE OF FREEMIUM:
Ah, freemium. The business model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:08.220
that has dominated the 21st century. Whether&nbsp;
you’re trying to consume media, trade stocks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:14.040
or stream music, you can do it all for free.&nbsp;
You just need an internet connection. This was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:19.020
one of the biggest revolutions brought on by&nbsp;
the internet. It made information, education,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:25.080
and building businesses more accessible than ever&nbsp;
before. But more recently, there seems to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:31.620
trend against free services. YouTube has managed&nbsp;
to garner 80 million paid subscribers. Apple Music&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:37.740
has managed to garner 78 million paid subscribers.&nbsp;
And even ProtonMail, a premium email client with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:42.960
paid tiers, has managed to garner 70 million&nbsp;
users. Each and every one of these services has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:48.720
a free alternative. Instead of YouTube premium,&nbsp;
you can just watch YouTube with ads. Instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:54.780
Apple Music, you can just stream free Spotify.&nbsp;
And instead of ProtonMail, you can use virtually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.780 --> 00:01:00.540
any other email client. But despite this, more&nbsp;
and more people are choosing to pay for these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:06.540
traditionally free services. In fact, it’s almost&nbsp;
become sort of a status symbol. Oh you still watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:12.060
ads on YouTube, what a peasant? This mentality&nbsp;
is especially prominent amongst the younger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:17.640
generation of high schoolers and young adults.&nbsp;
But it goes beyond just superficial looks. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:23.520
reality is that there is no such thing as a free&nbsp;
service. If you’re not paying with cash, you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:29.640
paying with attention, time, and/or privacy. And&nbsp;
as people become aware of this reality, the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.680
willing they are to reclaim their attention,&nbsp;
time, and privacy using cash. Companies are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:39.600
even starting to use this as a pitch as to why&nbsp;
users should pay for a service. ProtonMail for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:45.720
example proudly claims “Privacy isn’t free. Here’s&nbsp;
why that’s a good thing.” But, while I agree that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:50.580
reclaiming privacy is a definitely good thing&nbsp;
for customers, it’s not exactly a great thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:56.460
for businesses. And I’m not talking about Google&nbsp;
or Facebook. This is great for them. If they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:00.540
just directly monetize their billions of users,&nbsp;
they no longer have to be the bad guy that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:05.340
collecting and selling data. The companies that&nbsp;
this is bad for are the ones that are trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:11.100
advertise on these platforms. Suddenly, everyone&nbsp;
who’s well off or even middle class are impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:16.860
to reach making it exponentially harder to craft&nbsp;
successful ad campaigns. This could lead to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:23.760
pretty scary future for YouTube as advertisers&nbsp;
become more aggressive. So, join me as we take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:29.160
look at why free services are falling out of favor&nbsp;
and what this means for the freemium economy.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:39.300
NEVER WORTH IT:
The bottomline is that free services are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:43.800
never ever worth it. While it’s great that these&nbsp;
companies are able to offer their amazing services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:48.600
for free, if you have to means to pay for the ad&nbsp;
free version or the private version or whatever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:56.040
you should always take it. The reason is that free&nbsp;
services are terrible at monetizing users. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:02.280
platforms like Google and Facebook do make a crap&nbsp;
ton of money but that’s just because they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:08.160
mind bending levels of scale. On an individual&nbsp;
basis, these platforms make next to nothing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:14.040
And it’s no wonder why. There’s simply so much&nbsp;
friction when it comes to monetizing a free use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:20.160
base. Take YouTube for example. Let’s say that you&nbsp;
watch about 1 hour of YouTube per day. Nowadays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.560
you usually get an ad once every 3 minutes and&nbsp;
each ad is about 10 seconds so you’re watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:30.840
about a 100 minutes worth of ads every month. Let&nbsp;
me phrase that in a different way, by not getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:37.200
YouTube premium for $12/mo, you’re essentially&nbsp;
selling your time for $7.20 per hour or basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:43.740
minimum wage in the US. Also, that’s if you do&nbsp;
it stupidly. The smarter choice would obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:48.840
be to go for the family plan and split it with&nbsp;
your friends and family. The family plan costs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:54.840
$23 per month and allows for up to 6 members,&nbsp;
so each member is only paying about $4/mo. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.840 --> 00:04:00.780
by not getting YouTube premium, you’re actually&nbsp;
selling your time for $2.40/hour and that’s if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:05.100
you watch just 1 hour of YouTube per day. If you&nbsp;
watch 2 hours a day, you’re selling it for $1.2.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:10.560
If you watch 3 hours a day, you’re selling it for&nbsp;
just $0.80 cents. And if you’re a YouTube addict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:15.960
and watch 5 or 6 hours a day, you’re selling&nbsp;
it for just $0.40 to $0.50. Not to mention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:21.780
you’d be sitting through 10 hours of ads every&nbsp;
month. YouTube was an easy example but this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:27.420
the case with all free services. It’s impossible&nbsp;
for it to be any other way because of the physics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:33.420
of the situation. For a normal service to work,&nbsp;
only 2 parties have to be happy. The business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:38.820
and the user. For a freemium service to work,&nbsp;
three parties have to be happy. The business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:44.820
the user, and the monetizer. This is made worse&nbsp;
by the fact that most users don’t want anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:50.820
to do with the monetizer. I mean, who wants to&nbsp;
watch ads? This makes it especially difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:56.460
for the monetizer to make the transaction worth&nbsp;
their time. And by extension, it also makes it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:01.500
difficult for the business and the user to have&nbsp;
it be worth their time either. Back in the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:06.180
users didn’t think consider this inefficiency&nbsp;
too seriously. As far as they were concerned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:12.600
they were getting media or email or news or&nbsp;
whatever else completely for free. But nowadays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:17.220
if you don’t know how these platforms make&nbsp;
money, you’re definitely living under a rock.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:22.440
And people are simply coming to the logical&nbsp;
conclusion that giving up so much time, data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:27.540
and privacy just to save the $5 or $10 that&nbsp;
the service would’ve otherwise cost is stupid.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:34.560
Now again, it’s great that you can access such&nbsp;
great services for free but if you can afford,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:40.440
you should stay far far away from such deals as&nbsp;
they’re always a ripoff for everyone involved.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:47.700
SOCIETAL SHIFT:&nbsp;

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:52.860
So, that’s kind of the logical argument as to&nbsp;
why people are ditching free services but this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:58.500
shift is much deeper than a simple time vs money&nbsp;
relationship. The reality is that people simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:04.440
don’t trust free services. Back in the 2000s,&nbsp;
the average internet user might’ve thought: “Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:10.920
it’s just a website. How much could it possible&nbsp;
cost to run? That must be why its free.” But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:16.020
no one in their right mind thinks this today.&nbsp;
Everyone knows that internet companies are some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:22.260
of the most profitable and wealthiest companies&nbsp;
in the world. So, how exactly are they making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:27.180
this money and more importantly, where does&nbsp;
that leave the average user. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:31.860
the average user would be giving up time, data,&nbsp;
and privacy but I don’t even think that’s what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:37.380
annoys users the most. I think what annoys users&nbsp;
the most is the fact that they no longer have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:43.560
a voice because they’re no longer the customer.&nbsp;
They’re the product being sold while the monetizer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:48.900
is the real customer. This is what leads to the&nbsp;
disconnect between users and platforms that’s so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:54.180
common nowadays. Oh, how could YouTube remove&nbsp;
the dislike button. Why does YouTube censor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:59.580
great content just because its a little edgy. Why&nbsp;
did Robinhood halt GameStop. Do these companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:05.460
even listen to what their customers want? Well,&nbsp;
the answer is yes. These companies are very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:11.100
aware of what their customers want and they give&nbsp;
them just that. Advertisers want family friendly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:16.260
content that they can get behind. As such,&nbsp;
that’s what YouTube pushes to the top. At first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:21.180
users will complain about it but eventually&nbsp;
they’ll get over it. Something that they don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:26.640
get over though is the loss of trust. Users start&nbsp;
classifying these businesses as sell outs that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:31.440
don’t even care about their customers. But the&nbsp;
reality is that the only way that these businesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:37.080
could have done it differently is if their users&nbsp;
were also their customers. So, naturally, people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:42.840
have started to flock over to services where they&nbsp;
are the customer, where they feel valued. This has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:47.580
already started to become more of a status thing&nbsp;
actually and you can thank Apple for that. Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:53.820
is one of the only big tech companies who’s users&nbsp;
are actually their customers. And they’re very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:58.320
much leaning into this. Just take a look at all&nbsp;
of this marketing material about privacy. Privacy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:04.680
That’s iPhone. Privacy Matters. Privacy.&nbsp;
That’s Apple. What happens on your iPhone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:10.140
stays on your iPhone. All of this is actually very&nbsp;
similar to that ProtonMail slogan that we we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:15.240
talking about at the beginning of the video.&nbsp;
All of this enforces the idea that an ad free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:20.760
experience and data privacy come at a premium cost&nbsp;
which you can get at Apple. And let’s just say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:27.660
people are eating this up. The iPhone has barely&nbsp;
changed over the past 5 years but the same cannot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:32.580
be said about the iPhone’s marketshare. iPhone&nbsp;
marketshare has gone up substantially from about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:38.400
a third of the US market to over half of the&nbsp;
US market. And as Apple continues to grow their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:44.280
dominance, you can bet that people will only&nbsp;
be more inclined to pay to be the customer.

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:51.720
UNCERTAIN FUTURE:
So why are people ditching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:56.940
free services? Well, people have simply come to&nbsp;
the realization that free services aren’t exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.940 --> 00:09:02.460
free and people are very much fed up with free&nbsp;
services and their ulterior motives. Personally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:07.920
I think this is great thing for companies and&nbsp;
users. Companies make more money directly from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:12.360
the consumer and consumers get a better experience&nbsp;
while paying for exactly what they’re consuming.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:19.080
It makes perfect sense but it’s not great for&nbsp;
everyone. It’s especially bad for advertisers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:24.360
You see, advertisers have already been getting&nbsp;
destroyed by the new age of media. On YouTube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:29.280
we’re literally complaining about 3 minutes&nbsp;
of ads for every hour of content. On TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:34.800
it used to be like 12 minutes of ads for&nbsp;
every hour of content and it still is. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:40.020
just that no one really watches TV anymore. At&nbsp;
least, no one from the younger generations. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:45.180
advertisers were already fighting for 4x times&nbsp;
less ad space but ad free offerings are just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:50.160
making this exponentially worse. I mean,&nbsp;
who tends to pay for premium on YouTube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:55.560
or Hulu, or Netflix or really any platform.&nbsp;
Well, people who are well off and are able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.560 --> 00:10:01.200
spend a little more on entertainment. And who&nbsp;
is the most likely to convert from ads. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:04.920
people who are well off and are able to&nbsp;
spend a little more on entertainment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:11.280
So, these ad free offerings are literally removing&nbsp;
the most valuable audience from an already limited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:16.980
marketing pool. This leaves advertisers with only&nbsp;
one option. Target the content. This of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:22.500
means more companies doing sponsorships and paying&nbsp;
way more. We’ll also see more brand placement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:28.140
more affiliate marketing, more giveaways, more&nbsp;
brand deals, and all of the usual stuff but is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:33.540
that where it’ll end? I’m sure you’ve noticed one&nbsp;
common thread amongst all of these. They’re all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:39.360
skippable. Now, there are advantages to skippable&nbsp;
ads. Only people who are really interested in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:45.180
product will watch the ad and the advertisers can&nbsp;
get away with paying less. But, with that being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:51.240
said, some advertisers will always be looking for&nbsp;
a way to push unskippable ads. How would they be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:56.640
able to do this? Well, would YouTube ever push&nbsp;
out a feature that allows creators to show ads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:03.000
even to premium members? You’re probably thinking&nbsp;
that this would be blasphomous. Why the hell are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:08.400
you paying YouTube a premium price if you still&nbsp;
have to watch forced ads? Well, I don’t think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:13.380
YouTube would necessarily make money from these&nbsp;
ads. I don’t even think it would necessarily be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:18.240
feature that YouTube adds in on their own. Rather,&nbsp;
it could very well be a feature that creators ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:22.980
for due to how much brands are willing to pay.&nbsp;
They would probably justify it by saying that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:28.080
can make even better content if viewers watched&nbsp;
just one unskippable ad at the beginning of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:35.340
video. And then it’ll become 2 and 3 and 4 pretty&nbsp;
soon it’ll become just like mainstream TV. YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:40.440
will make money from the premium subscription just&nbsp;
like DirecTV or Dish Network. And then creators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:45.480
will make money from ads and infomercials&nbsp;
just like TV channels and their shows. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:50.580
is the trend towards premium subscriptions all&nbsp;
that great? Well, it really comes down to your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:55.500
personal preference. Would you rather see higher&nbsp;
quality content with more ads or lower quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.500 --> 00:12:01.620
content with less ads. Comment that down below.&nbsp;
Also, speaking of ads, YouTube recently blocked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:07.020
adblock. This might’ve been one of the worst&nbsp;
decisions yet. Check out this video to learn more.

